From b283483f4b8ecabdf48eaf5a8fb9dadfd0038c2e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 01:48:41 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Layout of the data at the integration points: parameter
 QuadratureDataLayout

The arrays of the data at the integration points of a material (states and
tangent operator blocks) can be stored point by point (default) or
component by component, the two layouts of MGIS. The second one gives
coalesced accesses on a GPU to the kernels using one thread per point. The
layout does not change the results.

- Parameter `QuadratureDataLayout` (`PointByPoint` or
  `ComponentByComponent`) of the non linear evolution problems (layout of
  their materials, default given by the environment variable
  `MFEM_MGIS_QUADRATURE_DATA_LAYOUT`), of `addBehaviourIntegrator` (layout
  of one material) and of `PointWiseModel`. Declared in
  `QuadratureDataLayout.hxx`, as `displacement_gradient_parameter`.
- `MultiMaterialNonLinearIntegrator` gives the layout of a material to its
  generator as the parameter `QuadratureDataLayout` when it is not the
  point by point layout: the generators of the users see no new
  parameter with the default layout.
- The generators of mfem-mgis read this parameter and give the layout to
  the constructors of the behaviour integrators (last argument, point by
  point by default), down to `Material`. The generators of the stationary
  heat transfer and of the micromorphic damage take the current signature
  of the generators. The code generator of the integrators is updated in
  the same way (its output differs from the tracked files, which were
  edited afterwards: they are edited, not regenerated).
- A material stored component by component requires the integration by
  batches and the computations of the finite elements by kernels (or the
  tangent operator applied without assembly): the integration point by
  point and the computations element by element read the values of a
  point as contiguous. They are refused at the setup of the problem
  (`checkPointByPointIntegrationSupport`,
  `checkElementByElementComputationsSupport` of
  `MultiMaterialNonLinearIntegrator`), and the element by element methods
  of the two CRTP bases of the behaviour integrators (integration,
  gradients, inner forces, stiffness matrix of an element) throw an
  exception for such a material
  (`BehaviourIntegratorBase::checkElementByElementComputationsSupport`):
  a direct call can't give wrong values. The functions which compute
  element by element refuse such a material on all the processes before
  computing: `getLinearizedOperators` (also used by the prediction when the
  boundary conditions contribute to its operator) and
  `computeResultantForceOnBoundary` (only its selected materials). The
  loop of the latter over the selected materials is shared with the
  synchronization of their thermodynamic forces
  (`internals::applyToSelectedMaterials`).
- `computeMeanThermodynamicForcesValues` reads the thermodynamic forces
  through the views of MGIS: they were read point by point whatever the
  layout.
- An external state variable with several components computed by an
  evaluator, whose values are stored point by point, is refused for a
  material stored component by component: MGIS reads such values in the
  layout of the material.
- Documentation: user guide (computations by batches, glossary, behaviour
  integrators).
- `BatchIntegrationTest`: each case computed by batches and by kernels is
  also computed with the component by component layout, the elastic
  material of the mesh with two materials keeping the point by point
  layout (parameter of the material). Same unknowns, thermodynamic
  forces, forces in the global frame, predictions and integrals of the
  thermodynamic forces to the last bit when the computations are
  reproducible, to the rounding errors otherwise (atomic assembly on a
  GPU, MUMPS). The direct write in the states after an update goes
  through a view of MGIS. Refusals and invalid values are tested.
- `ExecutionSettingsTest` and `UniaxialTensileTest` read the states of the
  materials through the views of MGIS instead of their raw arrays, so
  that they also check the component by component layout when it is
  selected by the environment.
